"Rainbow-partition conjecture for meandric partners | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/336857/rainbow-partition-conjecture-for-meandric-partners)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Rainbow-partition conjecture for meandric partners ... Goulden, Alexandru Nica and Doron Puder, “Asymptotics for a Class of Meandric Systems, via the Hasse Diagram of NC(n)”, arXiv:1708.05188 (2020).\n\nRainbow-partition conjecture for meandric partners\n\nAbout 9 years old · traced to 2017\n\nLet $n$n be a positive integer. A meandric partner of a partition $\\pi\\in\\mathrm{NC}(n)$π∈NC(n) is a partition $\\rho\\in\\mathrm{NC}(n)$ρ∈NC(n) such that the associated meandric system $M(\\pi,\\rho)$M(π,ρ) is a meander, equivalently such that $d_H(\\pi,\\rho)=n-1$d H​(π,ρ)=n−1. Let $r_n$r n​ be the rainbow partition of $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}${1,…,n}, whose blocks are $\\{1,n\\},\\{2,n-1\\},\\ldots${1,n},{2,n−1},…, together with a possible singleton block at $(n+1)/2$(n+1)/2 when $n$n is odd. Let $K$K denote the Kreweras complementation map, and consider the orbit $\\{K^j(r_n):0\\leq j<n\\}${K j(r n​):0≤j<n}. Rainbow-partition conjecture. The orbit of $r_n$r n​ under Kreweras complementation is exactly the set of partitions in $\\mathrm{NC}(n)$NC(n) having the largest number of meandric partners. Numerical experiments support this conjecture, which was also motivated by an idea raised in earlier work. By symmetry, the $n$n partitions in the orbit have the same number of meandric partners; determining whether there are any others with the same maximum remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nI. P. Goulden, Alexandru Nica and Doron Puder, “Asymptotics for a Class of Meandric Systems, via the Hasse Diagram of NC(n)”, arXiv:1708.05188 (2020).\n\nmeandric-systemsnoncrossing-partitions\n\n0 0\n\n3 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the